oran-dhcp-gen (2.8.5) stable; urgency=medium

  * The Lab4 TLS model's CA/RA profile is now confirmed end to end. The last
    two values still inherited from the stale `tls_ca:` sketch -- uri_path
    /pkix/ and app_protocol http -- were checked against the running CA: a
    plain-HTTP GET to http://[fd00:8b36:f2a9::2c:6]:8080/pkix/ is answered by
    the CMP responder with "405 CMP requires POST", which only that path and
    that scheme produce. A wrong path would 404.
    Nothing in that profile is guesswork any more, and the file header says so.
  * The dual-stack IPv6 rule recorded in 2.8.4 is confirmed by the O-RU
    itself, not just asserted. A 44R14 on Lab04 received both chains and wrote
    the v6 values to its dhcp_scan_file.json:
      ca_ra_ip_address "[fd00:8b36:f2a9::2c:6]", port 8080, protocol_flag 1
    Its own decode of the option 17 chain matches the generated model
    sub-option for sub-option, and the vendor option length it reported
    (152 = 4 enterprise + 148) is exactly what --explain predicts.

 -- labuser <labuser@dhcp-server>  Mon, 25 Aug 2026 21:50:00 +0000

oran-dhcp-gen (2.8.4) stable; urgency=medium

  * Recorded a domain rule that changes how a deployment should be verified:
    a dual-stack O-RU calls home over IPv6. When a class serves both families
    the O-RU takes an address on each and the IPv6 chain governs call-home, so
    option 17 is the operative payload and option 43 is not what the radio
    acts on.
    Three consequences worth having written down. For such a class the IPv6 CA
    address, IPv6 controller and the ipv6 entry of a per-family ca_ra.port are
    the values that reach the radio. A per-family port mapping is therefore
    decided by its ipv6 entry. And a DHCPv4 capture -- which is what confirmed
    the Lab04 deployment in 2.8.3 -- proves the generator emitted what was
    asked for, but says nothing about the path actually in use; confirming a
    dual-stack deployment needs a DHCPv6 capture (port 546 or 547) decoded
    against --explain's ipv6 section.
    Documentation only; no generated output changes. It is recorded because
    the verification claims in this changelog read stronger than they are
    without it.
  * The user guide's version table was missing rows for 2.8.2 and 2.8.3; added.

 -- labuser <labuser@dhcp-server>  Mon, 25 Aug 2026 19:05:00 +0000

oran-dhcp-gen (2.8.3) stable; urgency=medium

  * FIX the Lab4 TLS model carried two wrong values, and 2.8.2 wrongly
    recorded one of them as confirmed. Lab04 deployed the model and a tcpdump
    of the DHCP Offer to a 44R14 was decoded against it:

        0x03 CA/RA port    model 8091   wire 8080
        0x05 subject DN    model /OU=SPM/O=1Finity/C=US/CN=ATT-SSL/...
                           wire  /CN=1FinityLab Root CA/OU=WV Lab/...

    Both came from the commented-out `tls_ca:` block in oran_dhcp.yaml, which
    is stale in every value it recorded: the CA address was wrong (fixed in
    2.8.2), and so were the port and the subject DN. uri_path and
    app_protocol came from the same block and happen to be right.
    2.8.2's header claimed the port was "confirmed against the running CMP
    endpoint" on the strength of a report that it had been checked; the
    capture shows it had been checked and corrected, not confirmed. Recording
    a value as verified on second-hand wording is the same failure this tool
    exists to prevent, one level up.
  * The model is now byte-identical to what Lab04 serves: the generated
    option 43 for ATTn77 matches the 108 bytes captured on br-mplane exactly.
    The header records that, and which two values had to move to get there.
  * The subject DN is now the same one Lab01 and Lab03 send, so all three
    labs share the 1FinityLab root CA.

