oran-dhcp-gen (2.8.1) stable; urgency=medium

  * NEW References/kea/Lab4/oran_dhcp-tls.yaml -- the Lab4 model with NETCONF
    call-home over TLS actually turned on, rather than only made expressible.
    Everything the earlier releases added existed but was switched on nowhere
    a lab deploys from: Lab4 was still every class on ssh with no CA/RA
    profile at all.
    The CA/RA settings are recovered from the commented-out 'tls_ca:' block
    that shipped in oran_dhcp.yaml beside it -- CA IPv6 fd00:8b36:f2a9::95:240,
    port 8091, /pkix/, the SPM subject DN, http. That block recorded no IPv4
    CA address, so ca_server_ipv4 is a clearly marked placeholder and the file
    says so at the top; run --explain and read the 0x01 line before deploying.
    It is a separate file rather than a rewrite of oran_dhcp.yaml, which
    records what the lab runs today and is a regression-test input.
    The catch-all opts out of both the CA/RA block and 0x87 -- an unrecognised
    O-RU has no business being pointed at a CA -- so its chain is byte-identical
    to the SSH model, which a test pins.
  * FIX a malformed address anywhere in the model surfaced as a raw
    ipaddress.AddressValueError traceback instead of an [ERROR] line. It
    reached ipaddress inside a TLV builder, well past validation, so nothing
    said which field was wrong. Controller and CA/RA addresses are now parsed
    during validate() and a bad one dies naming the field:

      [ERROR] ca_ra_profile 'att_spm' ca_server_ipv4: 'REPLACE-ME' is not a
              valid ipv4 address (Expected 4 octets in 'REPLACE-ME')

    This matters more than it looks: the new TLS model ships a placeholder
    address on purpose, and a placeholder that tracebacks is a placeholder
    nobody can act on.

