oran-dhcp-gen (2.8.0) stable; urgency=medium

  * NEW sub-option 0x02, CA/RA server FQDN, via a ca_ra_profile's
    'ca_server_fqdn'. It reaches the CA by name and serves both families from
    one value, so a profile can drop its per-family addresses entirely:

        ca_ra_profiles:
          onefinity_ca:
            ca_server_fqdn: "ca.mplane.local"
            uri_path:       "/pkix/"
            subject_dn:     "/CN=1FinityLab Root CA/..."
            app_protocol:   "http"

    Setting both an address and a FQDN emits 0x01 then 0x02, in code order.
  * A ca_ra_profile must now offer at least one of ca_server_ipv4,
    ca_server_ipv6 or ca_server_fqdn, and an address-only profile must carry
    the address family every referring class actually serves. Before, an
    IPv4-only profile referenced by a dual-stack class produced a v6 CA/RA
    chain with no CA address in it -- a config that validates and cannot
    enrol.
  * Audited the emitter's sub-option coverage and wrote the result into the
    user guide, with the basis for each mapping stated rather than asserted.
    Nine of the ten codes are verified against working lab configs: Lab03 is
    decisive for 0x01 vs 0x81 because its CA and controller are different
    hosts, and it carries 0x82 as the named alternative to 0x81.
    0x02 is the exception and is documented as inferred, not read from the
    spec: it fills the only gap in the 0x01-0x06 CA/RA run and mirrors 0x82
    against 0x81. O-RAN.WG4.MP.0 clause 6.2.5 could not be retrieved to
    confirm it, and one public summary of an early revision describes the low
    codes as controller rather than CA/RA information -- a reading that
    contradicts how both labs use 0x01, and most likely describes a revision
    in which option 43 carried controller information only. Confirm 0x02
    against the specification before relying on it in production.
    Nothing claims the ten codes are the complete set.
  * The reference models deliberately do not use 0x02, so no shipped example
    endorses an unverified mapping; it is covered by inline test models only.

 -- labuser <labuser@dhcp-server>  Mon, 25 Aug 2026 14:55:00 +0000

oran-dhcp-gen (2.7.0) stable; urgency=medium

  * NEW --explain prints what each O-RU class will actually receive and writes
    nothing:

        oran-dhcp-gen oran_dhcp.yaml --explain

    Per class and family: the vendor-class prefix it matches, its pool range,
    and the option 43 / option 17 chain decoded sub-option by sub-option --
    CA/RA server and port, URI path, subject DN, controller IP, call-home mode
    and port -- followed by the flat hex so it can be compared against a
    capture. Until now the only way to see what a class sends was to read a hex
    chain out of the generated config by hand, or to put an O-RU on the wire
    and run tcpdump.
    The report is built on resolve_suboptions(), the same structured form the
    Kea backend consumes and which is byte-checked against the ISC builders
    before anything is emitted, so it cannot disagree with what is generated.
    test_explain_wire_matches_the_generated_config decodes both and compares
    them for every class and family in all three reference models.
    It also flags what a byte dump alone would not: a TLS class sending no
    0x87 is called out with the port the O-RU will fall back to, and a TLS
    class with no ca_ra block is noted as controller-only.
    --explain cannot be combined with --deploy or --restart.

 -- labuser <labuser@dhcp-server>  Mon, 25 Aug 2026 14:05:00 +0000

oran-dhcp-gen (2.6.0) stable; urgency=medium

  * NEW top-level 'defaults:' block. Every oru_class inherits controller,
    protocol, callhome_port, lease_profile, ca_ra and options from it unless it
    sets its own, so switching a lab between SSH and TLS is one edit instead of
    one edit per class:

        defaults:
          controller: ctrl
          protocol: tls
          callhome_port: auto
          ca_ra:
            profile: onefinity_ca

    Turning that 'protocol' line back to ssh drops every class from the full
    CA/RA chain to a bare controller IP. Previously the same change meant
    editing protocol, callhome_port and ca_ra on every class in the file, and
    the failure mode was missing one -- a single class still on ssh, or still
    on tls, looking exactly like the others in the YAML.
    Keys a class sets always win, and presence is what counts, not truth:
    'ca_ra:' with no value on a class overrides an inherited block with
    nothing. 'ca_ra' merges one level deep, so a class needing only its own
    port keeps the inherited profile. A CA/RA block is not inherited into an
    SSH class, which would otherwise make every SSH class in a TLS-by-default
    lab warn about a block it never asked for. 'defaults:' rejects keys that
    define a class rather than configure it -- name, match_prefix, the ranges.
  * NEW a class with 'protocol: tls' and no 'callhome_port' now warns. This is
    the failure sub-option 0x87 exists to prevent: 0x86 tells the O-RU to use
    TLS, nothing tells it which port, so it falls back to its firmware default
    -- 4334, the SSH call-home port, on the Fujitsu units. TLS call-home never
    completes, the lease looks healthy, and both dhcpd -t and kea-dhcp4 -t
    pass, so it reads as an O-RU fault. Both TLS classes in the Lab01
    reference are in exactly this state and were silent until now.
    A warning rather than an error: firmware that already defaults to 4335 is
    fine, and refusing would break configs that work today.
  * The Lab01 reference model now demonstrates 'defaults:' -- its classes
    spell out only what differs. Generated output is unchanged.

 -- labuser <labuser@dhcp-server>  Mon, 25 Aug 2026 12:55:00 +0000

oran-dhcp-gen (2.5.0) stable; urgency=medium

  * NEW ca_ra.port accepts a per-family mapping, not only a scalar:

        ca_ra:
          profile: onefinity_ca
          port:
            ipv4: 8081
            ipv6: 8080

    Lab01 reaches the same CMP endpoint on a different port per family --
    TDDn77 on v4 8081 / v6 8080, FDDn25n66 on v4 8083 / v6 8082 -- which the
    scalar form could not express: one value went into sub-option 0x03 for
    both families, so v6 clients would have been sent the v4 port. A scalar
    still means "same port for both" and is unchanged. A mapping that omits a
    family the class actually serves is rejected rather than defaulted, since
    silently falling back reintroduces the same wrong-port failure.
  * NEW References/isc/Lab01 -- the hand-written dhcpd.conf and dhcpd6.conf a
    second lab ran, plus the oran_dhcp.yaml that reproduces them. Unlike Lab03
    this lab puts the CA/RA endpoint on the controller host itself and sends
    /pkix/ rather than the EJBCA CMP path.
  * NEW test_generator_reproduces_lab01_vendor_chains decodes the option-43 and
    option-17 chains out of those hand-written files and requires the generated
    ones to match per class per family. Comments, logging and line breaks are
    allowed to differ; the bytes that reach an O-RU are not.
  * FIX the test helpers that decode ISC vendor chains matched the first
    "option vendor-encapsulated-options" text anywhere in a class block, so a
    commented-out alternative chain above the live one was decoded instead --
    Lab01's FDDn25n66 v6 block keeps its SSH variant commented directly above
    the TLS one, and the helper read 0x0081 where the wire carries 0x0001.
    Both decoders are now anchored to the start of a line.

 -- labuser <labuser@dhcp-server>  Mon, 25 Aug 2026 02:40:00 +0000

oran-dhcp-gen (2.4.0) stable; urgency=medium

  * NEW callhome_port on an oru_class emits O-RAN sub-option 0x87, the NETCONF
    call-home port. Takes an integer or 'auto', which resolves to the RFC 8071
    port for the class protocol -- SSH 4334, TLS 4335. Until now a class could
    say "use TLS" (sub-option 0x86 = 0x01) but had no way to say which port, so
    the O-RU fell back to its firmware default. On the Fujitsu units in the lab
    that default is 4334, the SSH call-home port: the O-RU opened a TLS
    connection to the SSH listener and call-home never completed. The
    hand-written Lab03 ISC configs carried 0x87 (87:02:10:ee) in their
    alternate option chains; the generator could not express it in either
    backend.
    The field is opt-in. A model that does not set it emits no 0x87 and its
    wire bytes are byte-identical to 2.3.0 -- upgrading does not change what a
    deployed lab sends.
  * FIX Kea DHCPv4 dropped T1/T2. A class on a lease_profile with renewal_time
    and rebinding_time got those as options 58/59 in the ISC class block, but
    the Kea config carried only the lifetimes, so the two backends renewed on
    different schedules -- for exactly the short bring-up profiles the TLS
    enrolment classes use. Kea has no per-client-class renew-timer/rebind-timer,
    so they are now emitted as class option-data the way ISC emits them.
    DHCPv6 is unaffected: T1/T2 are IA_NA fields there, not options.
  * FIX 'tls_ca:' under a controller is now a hard error naming ca_ra_profiles.
    It was never an implemented field, but a commented-out tls_ca block shipped
    in the Lab4 reference YAML, so uncommenting it read as the way to turn on
    TLS bootstrap. The generator ignored it and emitted a payload with no CA/RA
    sub-options, silently. Unknown fields on a controller, a class or a ca_ra
    block are now reported as warnings for the same reason.
  * The Lab4 reference YAML documents the three edits that turn a class from
    SSH to TLS call-home, in place of the tls_ca sketch it used to carry.
  * Tests: 0x87 must agree between the two backends and match the protocol
    (a TLS class emitting port 4334 fails), lab4 must stay free of 0x87 so the
    opt-in claim is checked, Kea and ISC must agree on T1/T2, and both tls_ca
    and an out-of-range callhome_port must be rejected without a traceback.

 -- labuser <labuser@dhcp-server>  Mon, 24 Aug 2026 22:40:00 +0000

oran-dhcp-gen (2.3.0) stable; urgency=medium

  * NEW --no-timestamp: omit the generation time from config headers so that
    output is a pure function of the input YAML. Without it every diff against
    a reference config showed a spurious header line and the regression check
    degraded into "ignore line 4".
  * FIX malformed or empty YAML raised a bare traceback out of yaml.safe_load
    instead of the [ERROR] message every other input problem produces.
  * Repository restructured. The source used to live at
    pkg/usr/local/bin/oran-dhcp-gen -- a Debian staging path doubling as the
    source tree, which meant pkg/ was both input and output and could not be
    cleaned, and the changelog and man page were committed gzipped and so were
    undiffable in git. Now: bin/ (source), packaging/debian/ (plain-text
    changelog and man page, gzipped at build time), tests/, examples/, docs/.
    pkg/ is gone; build output goes to build/ and dist/.
  * NEW packaging/debian/build-deb.sh builds the .deb and substitutes the
    version from __version__ into control, postinst and the man page. The
    version previously lived in five hand-edited places and had drifted: the
    shipped 2.2.3 man page said 2.2.2, and its postinst announced "What's new
    in 2.2.2" under a "v2.2.3 installed" header. The build now also refuses to
    run if the changelog does not lead with __version__, and generates the
    postinst "what's new" banner from the top changelog entry.
  * NEW test suite (tests/run_tests.py, runs with or without pytest). Golden
    files for both reference inputs, plus checks that ISC and Kea emit the same
    O-RAN sub-option codes per class, that every Kea vendor option-def is typed
    rather than a pre-built blob, and that sub-option 0x81 appears at the top
    level -- the three properties whose absence let the 2.2.0-2.2.2 option-43
    defect ship three times while kea-dhcp4 -t validated cleanly.

 -- labuser <labuser@dhcp-server>  Mon, 24 Aug 2026 20:15:00 +0000

oran-dhcp-gen (2.2.3) stable; urgency=high

  * FIX Kea backend option-43/17 encoding (regression introduced in 2.2.0).
    The vendor payload was emitted as a pre-built binary TLV blob nested
    under sub-option code 1; Kea wraps any vendor-space option-data in
    that sub-option's own TLV header, producing on the wire:
      v4: 01 LL <payload>            instead of   81 04 <ip> [...]
      v6: 00:01 LLLL <payload>       instead of   00:81 0010 <ip6> [...]
    O-RU firmware walking top-level sub-options finds unknown code 0x01,
    skips it, never learns the controller IP -> DHCP lease OK but no
    NETCONF call-home. Configs validated cleanly, so the defect was
    invisible to kea-dhcp4/6 -t. Verified empirically against Kea 2.4.1
    with live DHCPDISCOVER/OFFER capture: pre-fix wire bytes
    01 06 81 04 c0 a8 2c 06, post-fix 81 04 c0 a8 2c 06.
  * Kea backend now emits typed option-defs for each O-RAN sub-option code
    in use (0x01 ca-server-ip, 0x03 ca-port, 0x04 uri-path, 0x05 subject-dn,
    0x06 app-protocol, 0x81 netconf-server-ip, 0x82 mplane-fqdn,
    0x86 netconf-mode) and per-class typed option-data. Kea builds the
    TLVs; wire bytes match ISC output byte-for-byte (verified for simple
    SSH, simple TLS, and full CA/RA chains).
  * DHCPv6: explicit vendor-opts (option 17) container with enterprise id
    forced via always-send, guaranteeing emission independent of client ORO.
  * NEW: generation-time cross-check. Structured (Kea) sub-options are
    re-encoded to raw bytes and compared against the ISC chunk builders'
    output for every class/family; any divergence aborts generation.
  * Commas in string sub-option values are escaped for Kea option-data.
  * ISC output unchanged byte-for-byte except the version line.

 -- labuser <labuser@dhcp-server>  Fri, 21 Aug 2026 18:20:00 +0000

oran-dhcp-gen (2.2.2) stable; urgency=low

  * Multi-line ISC TLV emission. Vendor option chains with 3 or more
    sub-options now render with each sub-option on its own continuation
    line (matching the visual structure of pre-2.2.0 hand-edited
    dhcpd.conf / dhcpd6.conf files). Chains with 1 or 2 sub-options
    remain single-line. ISC dhcpd accepts both forms identically; this
    is a presentation change only — wire bytes are unchanged.
  * Refactored TLV builders to operate on per-sub-option byte chunks
    rather than flat byte strings. Kea backend continues to receive
    flat bytes via b''.join(chunks) (no behavioural change for Kea).

 -- labuser <labuser@dhcp-server>  Mon, 04 May 2026 18:30:00 +0000

oran-dhcp-gen (2.2.1) stable; urgency=medium

  * FIX: O-RAN sub-option 0x86 (call-home protocol) byte now auto-derived
    from class.protocol, per O-RAN WG4 M-Plane R004 §6.2.5:
      tls -> byte 0x01 (NETCONF/TLS)
      ssh -> byte 0x00 (NETCONF/SSH) [or omitted, both equivalent]
    Previous 2.2.0 emitted 0x00 for TLS classes by default — this was
    standards-non-compliant (signalled NETCONF/SSH for a TLS bootstrap)
    and was inherited from a stale value in pre-2.2.0 hand-edited configs.
  * DEPRECATED: ca_ra_profiles[].netconf_mode_byte. Field accepted as an
    override but emits a warning. Remove it to use the spec-compliant
    auto-derived value.

 -- labuser <labuser@dhcp-server>  Mon, 04 May 2026 18:00:00 +0000

oran-dhcp-gen (2.2.0) stable; urgency=low

  * NEW: lease_profiles top-level section. Define a named lease parameter
    set once (default_lease_time, max_lease_time, preferred_lifetime,
    renewal_time, rebinding_time) and reference it from any class via
    `lease_profile: <name>`.
  * NEW: ca_ra_profiles top-level section. Define a named TLS CA/RA
    bootstrap set once (ca_server_ipv4/v6, uri_path, subject_dn,
    app_protocol, netconf_mode_byte) and reference it from a class via
    `ca_ra: { profile: <name>, port: <N>, mplane_fqdn: ..., ... }`.
  * NEW: per-class ipv4_range and ipv6_range are now BOTH optional. Omit
    a range to disable that family for that class. At least one must be
    present.
  * NEW: shared pool emission. When two or more classes have identical
    ipv4_range/ipv6_range, the generator emits one pool block with multiple
    `allow members of "<class>";` statements (matches ISC idiom).
  * FIX: lease parameters now emitted at POOL scope (subnet/pool) where
    they actually take effect in ISC DHCP. The previous practice of placing
    `max-lease-time` / `preferred-lifetime` inside class blocks was a silent
    no-op. T1/T2 (option dhcp-renewal-time, dhcp-rebinding-time) remain at
    class scope where DHCP options correctly inherit through class membership.
  * Generated dhcpd.conf, dhcpd6.conf, kea-dhcp4.conf, kea-dhcp6.conf are
    wire-byte equivalent to the v2.1.x output for any existing v2.1.x YAML
    that does not use the new lease_profiles or ca_ra_profiles features.

